Prime number algorithm can also be done much more efficiently if we use the complexity O(sqrt(n))

Algorithm 1: O(n)

const isPrimeNum = (num: number): boolean => {
  if (num <= 1) return false;
  for (let i = 2; i < num; i++) {
    if (num % i === 0) return false;
  }
  return true;
}


Algorithm 2: O(sqrt(n))

const isPrimeNum = (num: number): boolean => {
  if (num <= 1) return false;
  if (num <= 3) return true;

  if (num % 2 === 0 || num % 3 === 0) return false;

  for (let i = 5; i * i <= num; i += 6) {
    if (num % i === 0 || num % (i + 2) === 0) return false;
  }

  return true;
};

This second algorithm is much more efficient, especially for large numbers, because the time it takes to run the algorithm grows with the square root of the number.

If a number "n" can be divided by some number  "d" then there must be another number "k" such that "d * k = n". These divisors "d" and "k" are paired.

If "n" is divisible by a number greater than its square root, the corresponding divisor would be smaller than the square root. For example, if "n" is divisible by 12, the pair divisors are 2 and 6, where 2 is smaller than the square root of 12 (~3.46).

If "n" has no divisors up to its square root, then it doesn’t have divisors greater than its square root (except in pairs where both divisors are either smaller or larger than the square root).

So if you check a very large number like 999999921539 you will see that the second algorithm is very fast, while the first one actually crashes at certain times.

